# Deploying a Three-Tier E-Commerce App on AKS

## 1. Project Overview — Stan's Robot Shop

The project used in this demo is **Stan's Robot Shop** — an open-source microservices demo application originally created by IBM/Instana. It simulates a real e-commerce website that sells robots.

**Repository:** [github.com/iamveeramalla/three-tier-architecture-demo](https://github.com/iamveeramalla/three-tier-architecture-demo) *(forked with AKS-specific fixes)*

### Run locally

```bash
git clone <repo-url>
cd three-tier-architecture-demo
docker compose up
```

Access at `http://localhost` to explore the full user workflow before deploying to Kubernetes.

---

## 2. Application Architecture & Microservices

### User workflow

1. Register an account → login
2. Browse product categories (AI / Robots)
3. Select a product → view rating → add to cart
4. Proceed to checkout → shipping cost calculated by location
5. Confirm order

### Microservices

| Service | Language | Role |
|---|---|---|
| **web** | HTML/Angular | Frontend — serves the UI via a web server (Nginx/httpd) |
| **user** | Node.js | User registration, authentication, session management |
| **catalogue** | Node.js | Product listing, categories, descriptions, images |
| **cart** | Node.js | Shopping cart — persists items between sessions |
| **shipping** | Java | Calculates shipping cost based on user location |
| **payment** | Python | Handles payment processing workflow |
| **dispatch** | Go | Order dispatch confirmation after successful payment |
| **ratings** | PHP | Product ratings — reads/writes to Redis |

### Databases and data stores

| Component | Type | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| **MongoDB** | Document DB (NoSQL) | `user` service — stores user accounts and credentials |
| **MySQL** | Relational DB | `catalogue` service — stores product data and images |
| **Redis** | In-memory data store | `ratings` service — stores and retrieves product ratings |

> Redis is created as a **StatefulSet** with a **PersistentVolume** — this ensures ratings data survives pod restarts.

### Architecture diagram

```
Browser
   │
   ▼
[web] ──────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │                                             │
   ├──► [user] ──► MongoDB                       │
   ├──► [catalogue] ──► MySQL                    │
   ├──► [cart] ──► Redis                         │
   ├──► [ratings] ──► Redis (StatefulSet + PV)   │
   ├──► [shipping]                               │
   ├──► [payment]                                │
   └──► [dispatch]                               │
                                                 │
[Ingress (App Gateway)] ◄───────────────────────┘
```

---

## 3. Why Different Databases?

**MongoDB for users:** User registration data (username, password, profile) is structured with relationships — MongoDB's document model suits this well.

**MySQL for catalogue:** Product data including images, descriptions, and pricing fits a relational model.

**Redis for ratings:** Ratings are read very frequently and updated in real-time by many users simultaneously. In-memory stores like Redis are significantly faster for this pattern than disk-based databases.

> Additionally, using multiple database types in one project gives DevOps engineers hands-on experience with containerising and managing different database workloads — a common real-world scenario.

---

## 4. Understanding the Dockerfiles

Each microservice has its own Dockerfile. The structure follows the same pattern regardless of language — only the base image and dependency commands differ.

### General Dockerfile pattern

```
1. FROM <language-base-image>
2. EXPOSE <port>
3. WORKDIR /app
4. COPY <dependency-file> .       (package.json / requirements.txt / go.mod / pom.xml)
5. RUN <install-dependencies>     (npm install / pip install / go mod download / mvn install)
6. COPY <source-code> .
7. CMD / ENTRYPOINT <start-command>
```

### Language-specific examples

| Language | Dependency file | Install command | Start command |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Node.js** | `package.json` | `npm install` | `node server.js` |
| **Python** | `requirements.txt` | `pip install -r requirements.txt` | `python app.py` |
| **Go** | `go.mod` | `go mod download` | run compiled binary |
| **Java** | `pom.xml` | `mvn package` | `java -jar app.jar` |
| **PHP** | `composer.json` | `composer install` | via web server |

### Multi-stage build (Java — shipping service)

The Java service uses a **multi-stage Dockerfile** to keep the final image small:

```dockerfile
# Stage 1: Build — heavyweight image with Maven
FROM debian AS build
RUN apt-get install -y maven
COPY pom.xml .
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn package -DskipTests

# Stage 2: Runtime — lightweight image, only the JAR
FROM openjdk:11-jre
WORKDIR /opt/shipping
COPY --from=build /target/shipping.jar .
CMD ["java", "-jar", "shipping.jar"]
```

**Why multi-stage?** The build stage includes Maven and all its dependencies (~hundreds of MB). The runtime stage only needs the compiled JAR (~MBs). The final image is dramatically smaller.

---

## 5. Helm Charts — Why and How

### Problem with plain YAML manifests

8 microservices + 2 databases + Redis = ~24+ YAML manifest files.

Across 3 environments (dev, staging, prod), this becomes 72+ files — many nearly identical except for a few values (image tag, resource limits, replica count).

### Helm solution

Helm uses three components:

| File / Folder | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Chart.yaml` | Metadata — chart name, version, description |
| `templates/` | All Kubernetes YAML manifests with **Jinja-style placeholders** for dynamic values |
| `values.yaml` | Default values that populate the placeholders in templates |

### How dynamic values work

In `templates/payments-deployment.yaml`:

```yaml
image: {{ .Values.image.repo }}/payments:{{ .Values.image.version }}
```

In `values.yaml`:

```yaml
image:
  repo: robotshop
  version: latest
```

At deploy time, Helm replaces `{{ .Values.image.repo }}` with `robotshop` and `{{ .Values.image.version }}` with `latest`.

To override for production:

```bash
helm install roboshop ./helm --set image.version=2.0.1 --namespace robot-shop
```

This changes the image tag across all manifests in one command — no editing of 24 files.

---

## 6. Setting Up the AKS Cluster

1. Azure Portal → **Kubernetes Service** → **Create**.
2. Settings:
- **Resource Group:** `e-commerce-demo`
- **Cluster preset:** Dev/Test
- **Cluster name:** `three-tier`
- **Region:** West US 2 (or nearest with available quota)
- **Availability zone:** Zone 1
3. Click **Review + Create** → **Create** (takes 10–15 minutes).

### Connect kubectl to the cluster

```bash
az aks get-credentials \
  --resource-group e-commerce-demo \
  --name three-tier \
  --overwrite-existing
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl config current-context
# Expected: three-tier
```

---

## 7. Deploying the Application with Helm

### Step 1 — Clone the repository

```bash
git clone <repo-url>
cd three-tier-architecture-demo
```

### Step 2 — Create the namespace

```bash
kubectl create namespace robot-shop
```

### Step 3 — Navigate to the AKS Helm folder

```bash
cd AKS/helm
```

### Step 4 — Install the Helm chart

```bash
helm install robot-shop . \
  --namespace robot-shop
```

### Step 5 — Verify all pods are running

```bash
kubectl get pods -n robot-shop
```

Expected: All pods in `Running` state across all 8 microservices + databases.

### Step 6 — Check services

```bash
kubectl get svc -n robot-shop
```

Note the `web` service port (typically `8080`) — needed for the Ingress configuration.

---

## 8. Persistent Volumes and Storage Classes on AKS

Redis is deployed as a **StatefulSet** with a **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)** to ensure data survives pod restarts.

### Why StatefulSet for Redis?

- Redis pods are ephemeral by nature — if the pod restarts, all in-memory data is lost.
- A PVC attaches persistent storage to the pod. When the new pod starts, it reconnects to the same volume and recovers all data.

### AKS Storage Classes

```bash
kubectl get storageclass
```

| Storage Class | Underlying Azure service | Use case |
|---|---|---|
| `default` (managed-csi) | Azure Managed Disk | Single pod access (ReadWriteOnce) |
| `azurefile` | Azure Files | Multiple pods / nodes sharing the same volume (ReadWriteMany) |
| `managed-premium` | Azure Premium SSD | High IOPS workloads |

### When to use each

| Scenario | Choose |
|---|---|
| One pod reads/writes to the volume (e.g., Redis, MySQL) | `default` (Azure Disk) |
| Multiple pods across different nodes share a volume (e.g., shared config files) | `azurefile` (Azure Files) |
| High-performance database workloads | `managed-premium` |

### Checking the PVC for Redis

```bash
kubectl get pvc -n robot-shop
```

The Redis PVC requests `1Gi` with storage class `default` — AKS automatically provisions an Azure Managed Disk.

```bash
kubectl get pvc -n robot-shop
# NAME           STATUS   VOLUME      CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   AGE
# data-redis-0   Bound    pvc-xxx     1Gi        RWO            default        5m
```

---

## 9. Ingress Controller — Azure Application Gateway

An Ingress controller provides:
- Path-based routing (`/cart` → cart service, `/payment` → payment service)
- Host-based routing
- TLS/SSL termination
- Web Application Firewall (WAF) capabilities
- Single external IP for all services

### Enable the Application Gateway Ingress Controller on AKS

1. Azure Portal → AKS cluster → **Networking** tab.
2. Find **Enable Ingress controller** → ✅ Check the box.
3. Click **Apply** (takes 5–10 minutes).

> This is significantly simpler than AWS EKS, where the Ingress controller must be installed manually via Helm and the AWS Load Balancer Controller must be configured separately.

### Verify the Ingress controller pods are running

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep ingress
```

Look for pods named `ingress-appgw-*` — status should be `Running`.

---

## 10. Writing the Ingress Resource

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: robot-shop-ingress
  namespace: robot-shop
spec:
  ingressClassName: azure-application-gateway
  rules:
- http:
        paths:
- path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: web
                port:
                  number: 8080
```

### Why specify `ingressClassName`?

A Kubernetes cluster can have **multiple Ingress controllers** (e.g., NGINX for one team, Azure App Gateway for another). The `ingressClassName` tells each Ingress resource which controller should handle it.

- If omitted, no controller picks it up (or the wrong one does).
- `azure-application-gateway` is the class name used by the AKS-managed App Gateway Ingress controller.

### Apply the Ingress

```bash
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml -n robot-shop
```

### Check the Ingress address

```bash
kubectl get ingress -n robot-shop
```

Once the external IP is assigned (may take 2–5 minutes), access the application at:

```
http://<INGRESS-EXTERNAL-IP>
```

---

## 11. Troubleshooting Notes

| Issue | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Pods stuck in `Pending` | Resource quota exceeded in the selected region | Change to a different Azure region with available quota |
| `ImagePullBackOff` | Image not accessible (private registry) | Add an image pull secret (see Day 15) |
| Ingress address not assigned | Ingress controller still initialising | Wait 5 minutes; delete the Ingress controller pod to force restart |
| `failed to list V1beta1 ApplicationGatewayRewrite` | Intermittent Ingress controller issue | Delete the `ingress-appgw` pod — it will restart automatically with a clean state |
| PVC stuck in `Pending` | No available storage class | Confirm `kubectl get storageclass` shows `default` as available |
| Redis pod crash-loops | PVC not bound | Check `kubectl describe pvc -n robot-shop` for provisioning errors |

---

## 12. Summary

| Concept | Key Takeaway |
|---|---|
| Microservices | Each service is independently deployable; language choice per service is optional but common in demo projects |
| Helm Chart | Parameterised Kubernetes deployment — `templates/` + `values.yaml` = reusable, environment-aware manifests |
| StatefulSet | Used for stateful workloads like Redis; guarantees stable pod identity and persistent storage across restarts |
| PVC + StorageClass | PVC requests storage; StorageClass defines the backend (Azure Disk, Azure Files) |
| Azure Disk (`default`) | Single-pod storage — ReadWriteOnce |
| Azure Files (`azurefile`) | Shared storage across pods/nodes — ReadWriteMany |
| Ingress | Routes external HTTP traffic to internal services; replaces NodePort/LoadBalancer per service |
| `ingressClassName` | Binds an Ingress resource to a specific controller — essential in multi-controller clusters |
| AKS App Gateway Ingress | Enabled via one checkbox in the AKS portal; no manual Helm install needed |

### Deployment commands reference

```bash
# Connect to AKS
az aks get-credentials --resource-group e-commerce-demo --name three-tier --overwrite-existing

# Create namespace
kubectl create namespace robot-shop

# Deploy with Helm
helm install robot-shop ./AKS/helm --namespace robot-shop

# Verify
kubectl get pods -n robot-shop
kubectl get svc -n robot-shop
kubectl get pvc -n robot-shop
kubectl get ingress -n robot-shop

# Apply Ingress
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml -n robot-shop

# Check storage classes
kubectl get storageclass
```

